<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:advisories="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels.Advisories"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="1000" d:DesignHeight="30"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.AdvisoryStripView"
             x:DataType="advisories:AdvisoryStripViewModel">
  <!--
    The editor's advisory strip (docs/design/handoff.md § "Editor shell", mockup 1e): an amber tint,
    a dot, one calm sentence and a bordered `Review N`.

    ONE STRIP, NOT FOUR. "One summary strip per tab" is satisfied by one control in one row whose
    sentence and count are the *open* section's — AdvisoryStripViewModel.AdvisorySummary and
    .AdvisoryCount are already narrowed to the selected tab (and, on the Layout tab, to the shown
    layer) by the editor, which projects the set onto it. Four copies would be four things to keep
    in step.

    IT NEVER BLOCKS AND IT NEVER MOVES THE BOARD. The row's space is RESERVED, not collapsed
    (issue #119): the Border is always on screen and always at least HeightAdvisoryStrip tall, and
    only its contents — the dot, the sentence and `Review N` — come and go with HasAdvisories. The
    strip used to hide itself whole, so an advisory arriving grew the row from 0 to 30 and pushed
    the board, the inspector rail and the legend row down a bar's worth mid-edit; holding the floor
    costs 30px of a screen that already budgeted them and moves nothing, ever. A bottom status bar
    would have freed the row, and was rejected: the handoff draws no bottom bar anywhere, and it
    would have parked the note far from the board it annotates. Nothing here is a gate either —
    `Review` selects what the note is about and changes nothing.

    RESERVED IS NOT THE SAME AS DRAWN. With nothing to say the strip paints nothing at all: no
    tint, no hairline, just the ground behind it. A permanently amber bar over a clean profile
    would be a standing false alarm — worse than the reflow it fixes. Both faces are class setters
    (`Border.advisoryStrip` and its `.hasAdvisories` state) rather than values on the element,
    because a local value beats a class setter and the clean face would never take — the same trap
    the sentence styles below already document.

    AMBER, NEVER RED. The fill, the border, the dot and the sentence are all on the StatusAdvisory
    ramp. An advisory is never painted on the StatusError ramp anywhere in the app.

    ONE PREFERENCE MOVES IT. `advisory_detail` ("Explain advisory warnings in full instead of one
    line", mockup 1j) flips the sentence from trimmed to wrapped, and the fixed 30px height becomes
    a MinHeight so the strip grows to fit. That is a layout change and nothing else: same tint,
    same dot, same Review, no new surface — and no animation, because growth here is a state the
    user asked for rather than a transition to sell (the motion budget is fixed and small).
    The trimming and the margin are set by the two styles below rather than inline, because a local
    value on the element would win over the class and the expanded state would never take.
  -->
  <UserControl.Styles>
    <!-- The reserved, unpainted face: what the user sees on a clean profile is the ground the
         strip sits on. Transparent rather than unset so the claim is one the styles state
         outright, and so a future setter cannot leave the bar half-painted. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.advisoryStrip">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The advisory face. The hairline is inside the 30px floor, so turning it on does not grow
         the bar by the pixel it draws. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.advisoryStrip.hasAdvisories">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,1" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.advisorySentence">
      <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="8,0,12,0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.advisorySentence.expanded">
      <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="None" />
      <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
      <!-- 6px top and bottom: one line still measures under the 30px minimum, so a short advisory
           does not jump when the preference is on, and only a sentence that really wraps grows. -->
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="8,6,12,6" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>

  <!-- Top, not stretched: MinHeight is a floor and the strip's height is otherwise its content's,
       so a host that hands it more room than it asked for must not inflate the bar. It carries no
       IsVisible: the bar holds its row whether or not there is anything in it. -->
  <Border Classes="advisoryStrip"
          Classes.hasAdvisories="{Binding HasAdvisories}"
          MinHeight="{DynamicResource HeightAdvisoryStrip}"
          VerticalAlignment="Top"
          Padding="24,0">
    <!-- The contents are what appears and disappears. Hidden, they measure nothing, so the empty
         bar is exactly its 30px floor. -->
    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto"
          IsVisible="{Binding HasAdvisories}">
      <!-- The dot is the strip's own mark: a filled disc rather than one of the three icon
           families, because it says "there is something here", not what kind of thing it is —
           the sentence beside it does that. -->
      <Ellipse Grid.Column="0"
               Width="8"
               Height="8"
               Fill="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryBrush}"
               VerticalAlignment="Center" />

      <!-- Sans, not mono: this is the app talking, not a value out of a config file. It trims to
           one line by default and wraps to its full text when `advisory_detail` is on. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                 Text="{Binding AdvisorySummary}"
                 Classes="meta statusWarning advisorySentence"
                 Classes.expanded="{Binding IsExpanded}"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />

      <!-- Bordered, as the handoff draws it. The padding and height are the strip's, not the
           button role's: SecondaryButton's own 13,8 would stand it taller than the 30px bar. -->
      <controls:AccessibleButton Grid.Column="2"
                                 Classes="secondary"
                                 Content="{Binding ReviewCaption}"
                                 Command="{Binding ReviewAdvisoriesCommand}"
                                 Padding="10,0"
                                 Height="22"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
    </Grid>
  </Border>
</UserControl>
